op7t-0197: Bluetooth from boot, in the kernel (bsd/dev/sweet_bt.c, kern_exec.c)

2026-11-11: Bluetooth works with the chip brought up by sysctl kern.sweetbt=1
from bt_up.sh, bluetoothd pointed at my kernel control by bthook's
BT_HCI_TRANSPORT + CTLIOCGINFO rewrite, and bthook editing the chip's replies
for bluetoothd's Broadcom expectations. It should come straight from the
image. This moves into the kernel:

- a thread from sweet_ufs_init (sweet_bt_init), after sweetbtdelay s (12):
  power pulses, firmware, NVM (what kern.sweetbt=1 did), io thread and
  kernel control; up to 3 tries, 10 s apart. kern.sweetbt=1 then says
  EALREADY (bt_up.sh waits for stage 6);
- the kernel control also registered as "com.sweet.bt", and bluetoothd
  started with BT_HCI_TRANSPORT=H4:POSIX:com.sweet.bt,115200 (kern_exec.c,
  next to sweetinject's variable), so its H4 driver takes the byte-stream
  path by itself;
- bthook's HCI fix-ups in the io thread: the chip's bytes cut into whole H4
  packets; Read Local Supported Commands gets octet 18 bit 3 (0x0C5B),
  0x0C5B's status -> 0, a vendor command's 4-byte "unknown command" reply
  -> status 0 + its first parameter byte + zeros (32 bytes); both parsers
  restart at every connect.

Stays userland: the BlueTool stand-in (bluetool_stub.py) and bthook's fix of
bluetoothd's empty H4 teardown slot. The old bthook keeps working on this
kernel (its edits no longer match; its name rewrite finds the Apple name,
still registered).
Boot-args: sweetbt=0 no boot bring-up, sweetbtdelay=N, sweetbtfix=0 no
edits, sweetbtenv=0 no BT_HCI_TRANSPORT.
Not compiled locally (no compiler on this PC): the CI build is the check.

Apply after 0196.
